<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a>–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a>, was an English statesman who served Charles II as Chancellor of the Exchequer and Lord Chancellor. One of the eight Lords Proprietors of Carolina, he drafted the Fundamental Constitutions of Carolina with his secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>. He later led the campaign to exclude the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James, Duke of York</a>, from the succession, helping to found the Whig party, and died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-ashley-cooper-1st-earl-of-shaftesbury.jpg" alt="Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Greenhill · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthony_Ashley-Cooper,_1st_Earl_of_Shaftesbury_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anthony Ashley-Cooper was born on 22 July 1621 at Wimborne St Giles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, the home of his maternal grandfather, Sir Anthony Ashley. He was the eldest son of Sir John Cooper, 1st Baronet, of Rockbourne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, and Anne Ashley. His mother died in 1628 and his father in 1630, which left him, at nine, a wealthy orphan and a baronet. In March 1640, still a minor, he was elected member of parliament for Tewkesbury. When the Civil War began in 1642 he first supported the King. In 1643 he raised a regiment of foot and a troop of horse at his own expense and became governor of Weymouth and Portland. In 1644 he joined the Parliamentarians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under the Commonwealth he sat on the committee on law reform chaired by Sir Matthew Hale and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> nominated him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barebones-parliament">Barebone's Parliament</a> as member for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-council-of-state">English Council of State</a>. In the elections of 1654 he led a slate of ten candidates in Wiltshire against republicans headed by Edmund Ludlow. He feared that Cromwell would rule through the Army rather than Parliament, and in January 1655 he stopped attending Council.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with Charles II in late May 1660 and was appointed to the Privy Council on 27 May. He sat on the commission that tried the regicides. He was Chancellor of the Exchequer from 1661 to 1672 and Lord Chancellor from 1672 to 1673, and one of five members of the Cabal ministry after the fall of the Earl of Clarendon. Charles created him Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a> in 1672. He invested in the Company of Royal Adventurers and the Royal African Company, serving as the latter's Sub-Governor in 1672 and 1673, and the two companies sent about 120 slaving voyages between 1663 and 1677 while he was associated with them. He also helped enshrine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the Fundamental Constitutions of Carolina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When it became widely known in 1673 that the King's brother and heir James had secretly become a Catholic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a>, like many English Protestants, saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> as linked to arbitrary government. His sponsorship of the Exclusion Bill in 1679 led to two years of struggle and ended in defeat. In 1681 he was arrested for high treason, though the prosecution was dropped months later. Fearing re-arrest, he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1682 and died there on 21 January 1683. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> called him a vigorous and indefatigable champion for civil and ecclesiastical liberty. The Ashley and Cooper rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charleston-south-carolina">Charleston, South Carolina</a>, are named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1663 Charles II granted the land between Virginia and Spanish Florida to eight Lords Proprietors, among them <b>Anthony Ashley Cooper</b> and the Earl of Clarendon.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-carolina">Province of Carolina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wimborne Minster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.8,-1.98&amp;year=1661" aria-label="Wimborne Minster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4893 5781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4893 5781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.8,-1.98&amp;year=1661">What was here in 1661? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
